<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Mathematical Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Bayesian Optimization with Gaussian Processes</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Gaussian process (GP) describes a collection of random variables, any finite number of which have a joint Gaussian distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. Mathematically, a GP can be expressed as, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{GP}\left(m(\mathbf{x}),k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\theta})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{GP}\left(m(\mathbf{x}),k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\theta})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean function and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariance (kernel) function parameterized by some hyperparameters <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given any finite collection of inputs <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{x}_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{x}_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the outputs are jointly Gaussian,</p>
<table id="A1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{X})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{m}_{\mathbf{X}},\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>𝐗</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{X})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{m}_{\mathbf{X}},\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{m}_{\mathbf{X}}=m(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>𝐗</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{m}_{\mathbf{X}}=m(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean function vector evaluated at <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, often assumed to be <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟎</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in practice, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariance matrix with entries <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})]_{i,j}=k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})]_{i,j}=k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the evaluations of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at any point <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are corrupted by a <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-sub-Gaussian noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{t}=f(\mathbf{x}_{t})+\epsilon_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{t}=f(\mathbf{x}_{t})+\epsilon_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the observed data <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}=\{\mathbf{X},\mathbf{y}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}=\{\mathbf{X},\mathbf{y}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{y}_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{y}_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the joint Gaussian distribution of the observed data and an arbitrary query point <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{y}\\
f(\mathbf{x})\end{array}\right]\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I}&amp;\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})\\
\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})&amp;k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x,\mathbf{x}})\end{array}\right]\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐲</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{y}\\
f(\mathbf{x})\end{array}\right]\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\left[\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I}&amp;\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})\\
\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})&amp;k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x,\mathbf{x}})\end{array}\right]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}=\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}=\mathbf{K}({\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X};\boldsymbol{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{k}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{k}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that, the posterior distribution of any query point <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is marginally Gaussian,</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t};\boldsymbol{\theta}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta}),\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t};\boldsymbol{\theta}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta}),\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E6.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t}]=\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I})^{-1}\mathbf{y}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t}]=\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I})^{-1}\mathbf{y},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E6.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[f(\mathbf{x})f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t}]=\mathbf{k}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I})^{-1}\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[f(\mathbf{x})f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t}]=\mathbf{k}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})(\mathbf{K}_{t;\boldsymbol{\theta}}+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\mathbf{I})^{-1}\mathbf{k}_{t,\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above posterior distribution, the acquisition function the use its statistics to trade-off exploitation (where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is high) and exploration (where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is high) effectively. Among the various acquisition functions proposed, expected improvement (EI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> remains the default choice in many BO applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. Let us define <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+}=\max_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}}\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+}=\max_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}}\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> as the best mean value. The EI acquisition function can then be expressed in closed form as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{D}_{t})=\mathbb{E}[\max\{0,f(\mathbf{x})-\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+}\}]=\sigma_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})[u\Phi(u)+\phi(u)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{D}_{t})=\mathbb{E}[\max\{0,f(\mathbf{x})-\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+}\}]=\sigma_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})[u\Phi(u)+\phi(u)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=(\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})-\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+})/\sigma_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(\mu_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})-\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}^{+})/\sigma_{t}(\mathbf{x};\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are the standard normal density and cumulative distribution functions, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Model Selection</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct model selection over a discrete, infinite space of kernels <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\{k_{1},k_{2},\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\{k_{1},k_{2},\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As each kernel comes with its own hyperparameters, we are actually dealing with a space of kernel families. Thus, when referring to a kernel <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the whole family over its hyperparameters <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\,|\,\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\Theta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\,|\,\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\Theta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given some model selection criteria <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to identify the optimal kernel,</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}=\arg\max_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\,h(k\,|\,\mathcal{D})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}=\arg\max_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\,h(k\,|\,\mathcal{D}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A commonly-used criterion for probabilistic models, such as GPs, is the marginal log-likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>,</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h(k\,|\,\mathcal{D})=\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},k)=\log\int p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},\boldsymbol{\theta},k)p(\boldsymbol{\theta})d\boldsymbol{\theta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(k\,|\,\mathcal{D})=\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},k)=\log\int p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},\boldsymbol{\theta},k)p(\boldsymbol{\theta})d\boldsymbol{\theta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, the above likelihood is generally intractable for GPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, so we resort to the Laplace approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},k)\approx\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}},k)+\log p(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-\frac{1}{2}\log\det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}+\frac{d_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}{2}\log 2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},k)\approx\log p(\mathbf{y}\,|\,\mathbf{X},\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}},k)+\log p(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-\frac{1}{2}\log\det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}+\frac{d_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}{2}\log 2\pi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimate of the hyperparameters with <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> being its dimension. The term <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}=-\nabla^{2}\log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}\,|\,\mathcal{D},k)|_{\boldsymbol{\theta}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}=-\nabla^{2}\log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}\,|\,\mathcal{D},k)|_{\boldsymbol{\theta}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the Hessian matrix evaluated at the MAP estimate. Note that Eq. (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.2 Model Selection ‣ Appendix A Mathematical Details ‣ Adaptive Kernel Design for Bayesian Optimization Is a Piece of CAKE with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) can be interpreted as rewarding model fit while penalizing model complexity. In this work, we use the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, which was also previously employed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and can be seen as an approximation of the Laplace method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide additional details on the implementation, baselines, and benchmarks employed in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Implementation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Context-Aware Kernel Evolution ‣ Adaptive Kernel Design for Bayesian Optimization Is a Piece of CAKE with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, CAKE depends on three key parameters: the number of crossovers <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the mutation probability <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the population size <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. To evaluate the impact of these parameters, we conduct a sensitivity analysis in Fig.  <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ B.1 Implementation ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Adaptive Kernel Design for Bayesian Optimization Is a Piece of CAKE with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, which shows the average fitness of the population over generations under different settings. Below, we provide some intuition for setting these parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of crossovers.</span>
The number of crossovers determines how many new candidate kernels are generated via crossover in each iteration. A higher value of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> can accelerate the exploration of diverse kernel combinations but increases the number of API calls to the LLM, which may be costly. In our experiments, we found that setting <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{c}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{c}=5</annotation></semantics></math> offers a good balance between exploration and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mutation probability.</span>
Each mutation operation introduces local variations to existing kernels in the population, which may help to refine solutions and escape local optima. The mutation probability controls the likelihood of applying a mutation operation during each iteration. A higher <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> promotes greater diversity and prevents premature convergence, especially when the top-performing kernels become similar. However, excessively high mutation rates risk disrupting promising kernel structures before they can be fully explored. Based on our experiments, we set <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}=0.7</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain a balance between sufficient exploration and stable evolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Population size.</span>
The population size dictates how many candidate kernels evolve simultaneously. A larger <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> enhances diversity in the search space and supports more thorough exploration, but also increases computational cost due to repeated GP model fitting for each kernel. Conversely, a smaller <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> may lead to premature convergence or insufficient sampling of the kernel space. Empirically, we found that setting <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{p}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{p}=10</annotation></semantics></math> maintains a diverse yet computationally manageable population.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17998v1/crossover_ablation.svg" id="A2.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/573;" width="476" height="573"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Number of crossovers.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17998v1/mutation_ablation.svg" id="A2.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/573;" width="476" height="573"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mutation probability.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17998v1/popsize_ablation.svg" id="A2.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/573;" width="476" height="573"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Population size.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average fitness (<math id="A2.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) with respect to different CAKE setup. The error bars indicate the standard errors.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test our proposed method, we consider three categories of baseline methods: <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fixed</span>, <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adaptive</span>, and <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compositional</span> approaches.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.1 </span>Fixed</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the fixed kernel-based baselines, we consider the following widely-used kernels in BO, along with their respective hyperpriors:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Squared Exponential (SE):</span></p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{2l^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{2l^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the lengthscale parameter and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplitude parameter. The hyperpriors are:</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E12.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E12.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Periodic (PER):</span></p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{\sin^{2}\left(\pi|x-x^{\prime}|/p\right)}{l^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{\sin^{2}\left(\pi|x-x^{\prime}|/p\right)}{l^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the lengthscale parameter, <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the period length, and <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplitude parameter. The hyperpriors are:</p>
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E14.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E14.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E14.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear (LIN):</span></p>
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}xx^{\prime}+\sigma_{c}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}xx^{\prime}+\sigma_{c}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance parameter, <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{c}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{c}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise variance. The hyperpriors are:</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E16.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E16.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{c}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{c}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rational Quadratic (RQ):</span></p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\left(1+\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{2\alpha l^{2}}\right)^{-\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\left(1+\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{2\alpha l^{2}}\right)^{-\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is the lengthscale parameter, <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative weighting parameter, and <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplitude parameter. The hyperpriors are:</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx9" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E18.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E18.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E18.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E18.3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matérn:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\frac{2^{1-\nu}}{\Gamma(\nu)}\left(\sqrt{2\nu}D\right)^{\nu}K_{\nu}\left(\sqrt{2\nu}D\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\frac{2^{1-\nu}}{\Gamma(\nu)}\left(\sqrt{2\nu}D\right)^{\nu}K_{\nu}\left(\sqrt{2\nu}D\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{l^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\frac{(x-x^{\prime})^{2}}{l^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is the distance between <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> scaled by the lengthscale parameter, <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is the modified Bessel function, and <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplitude parameter. In our experiments, the smoothness parameter <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5/2</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to Matérn-<math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> (M1), Matérn-<math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> (M3), or Matérn-<math id="A2.I1.i5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5/2</annotation></semantics></math> (M5) respectively. The hyperpriors are:</p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx10" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E21.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,2.0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E21.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E21.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Gamma</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\text{Gamma}(2.0,3.0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that all the above kernels are defined on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and are applied to input dimension <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> when indicated by the base kernel symbol, e.g., SE<sub id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sub> denotes SE kernel is applied to the <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.2 </span>Adaptive</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the adaptive kernel-based baselines, we adopt the implementation from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roman et al. [37]</a></cite> and apply the following selection criteria:</p>
<ol id="A2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random:</span> This criterion selects a kernel randomly from the set of available kernels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility:</span> Based on the proposed query points from each kernel, this criterion selects the kernel with the highest utility (acquisition) value:</p>
<table id="A2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}=\arg\max_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\,\alpha(\mathbf{x}_{t,k};\mathcal{D},k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}=\arg\max_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\,\alpha(\mathbf{x}_{t,k};\mathcal{D},k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BIC:</span> This criterion selects the kernel with the lowest BIC value:</p>
<table id="A2.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}=\arg\min_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\,\mathrm{BIC}(k;\mathcal{D})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>BIC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}=\arg\min_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\,\mathrm{BIC}(k;\mathcal{D}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.3 </span>Compositional</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the compositional kernel-based baselines, we consider the following methods:</p>
<ol id="A2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deep GP.</span> For the deep GP baseline, we use the <span id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepGP</span> implementation from GPyTorch, where training and inference are conducted using the doubly stochastic variational inference method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ensemble GP.</span> For the ensemble GP baseline, we follow the implementation suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. [31]</a></cite>, using the six base kernels used in CAKE to form the kernel dictionary.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compositional Kernel Search (CKS).</span> For the CKS baseline, we start from the same base kernels used in CAKE and apply greedy search to search for the kernel structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automated BO (ABO)</span> For the ABO baseline, we use the code provided by the authors at <a href="https://github.com/gustavomalkomes/abo" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/gustavomalkomes/abo</a> and follow the setup suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments on the benchmarks were conducted locally on a consumer-grade laptop<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            MacBook Air M2 (2022) with an 8-core CPU, 8-core GPU, 8 GB unified memory, and 256 GB SSD storage.</span></span></span>, except for the photonic chip design experiment in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Photonic Chip Design ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Adaptive Kernel Design for Bayesian Optimization Is a Piece of CAKE with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, which was executed on a high-performance computing (HPC) cluster due to the computational demands of the physics-based simulation.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.3.1 </span>Hyperparameter Optimization</h4>

<figure id="A2.T2" class="ltx_table">
